# Gentian OS installer non-secret configuration template
#
# Usage:
#   cp install.env.template install.env
#   edit install.env
#   ./install.sh
#
# install.sh auto-loads ./install.env when present. Values here are non-secret
# and may be committed if your organization allows it; prefer
# environment-specific private copies.
#
# THREE SURFACES, and this is only one of them:
#
#   install.env      how to RUN the install — which repositories to fetch from,
#                    which cluster directory this is, how the installer behaves.
#                    Per machine, never committed to gentian-deployments.
#   the Cluster claim
#                    what the cluster IS — domain, network and routing modes,
#                    issuer, mail, storage, whether it serves models. Scaffolded
#                    by `./install.sh --prepare-deployment` into
#                    gentian-deployments and edited there; authoritative from
#                    that moment on.
#   the prompts      credentials, and nothing else. Declared in credentials.yaml
#                    and stored in OpenBao.
#
# Four values below normally need changing; the rest are defaults for a fork,
# a mirror or a debugging session. Everything commented out is optional.
#
# Credentials are not configured here. install.sh reads credentials.yaml,
# prompts for the entries it declares as `phase: bootstrap`, and writes them to
# OpenBao. Set them in install.secrets.env to answer those prompts ahead of time
# for a non-interactive run: MASTER_PASSWORD, registry and SMTP credentials,
# and the optional CF_API_TOKEN and GENTIAN_DEPLOYMENTS_GIT_TOKEN.

# =============================================================================
# Repository sources
# =============================================================================
# Where this cluster's configuration and app catalogue come from.
#
# GENTIAN_*_AUTH selects how the installer authenticates to that repo:
#   none    public repo, no credential needed (the default for os/apps/ui)
#   basic   username + token/password, prompted at install time
#   bearer  a single bearer token, prompted at install time
# The credential itself is never set here — see credentials.yaml and the
# "Credentials" prompt install.sh runs before any step.
GENTIAN_APPS_REPO=https://github.com/gentian-org/gentian-apps
GENTIAN_APPS_BRANCH=main
GENTIAN_APPS_AUTH=none
GENTIAN_UI_REPO=https://github.com/gentian-org/gentian-ui
# develop, where gentian-apps is main: each repo's own deploying branch, which
# is not the same branch in both.
GENTIAN_UI_BRANCH=develop
GENTIAN_UI_AUTH=none
# Tier 0: the only one of the four that is optional: false in credentials.yaml
# (a private deployments repository cannot describe its own access), so this
# defaults to basic rather than none — an unset install.env keeps today's
# always-prompt behavior. Set to none explicitly for a public deployments repo.
GENTIAN_DEPLOYMENTS_REPO=https://github.com/gentian-org/gentian-deployments
GENTIAN_DEPLOYMENTS_BRANCH=main
GENTIAN_DEPLOYMENTS_AUTH=basic

# Where the platform itself comes from. Point these at a mirror for an
# air-gapped or forked install; the child ApplicationSets follow.
# GENTIAN_OS_BRANCH is the Git ref every in-cluster Application tracks — the
# kernel ApplicationSets, the operator Application, and the bootstrap
# Applications. Leave it empty to follow the checkout install.sh runs from,
# which is what a dev cluster wants; set a branch or release tag to pin a
# cluster, e.g. v0.4.0.
#
# Empty, not `main`: main trails the release branch by hundreds of commits, so a
# template that pre-filled it handed every new cluster a platform older than the
# installer running against it.
GENTIAN_OS_REPO=https://github.com/gentian-org/gentian-os
GENTIAN_OS_AUTH=none
GENTIAN_OS_IMAGE_REPOSITORY=ghcr.io/gentian-org/gentian-os
GENTIAN_OS_BRANCH=

# Identifies this cluster inside gentian-deployments — it is the directory name
# under clusters/, not the Kubernetes cluster name and not a kubeconfig context.
# Use the cluster's own ID, e.g. pck-cf2sw4h:
#   clusters/<GENTIAN_DEPLOYMENTS_CLUSTER_ID>/kernel
#   clusters/<GENTIAN_DEPLOYMENTS_CLUSTER_ID>/tenants/*
# There is no <stage> segment in the tenants path: a cluster has exactly one
# stage for its whole lifetime, so its tree is already implicitly that stage.
#
# On first bootstrap this also names the Cluster claim, as
# <GENTIAN_DEPLOYMENTS_CLUSTER_ID>-<GENTIAN_DEPLOYMENTS_STAGE>. Set it before
# the first run: scaffolding commits and pushes the tree it names to the shared
# deployments repo.
GENTIAN_DEPLOYMENTS_CLUSTER_ID=default-cluster
GENTIAN_DEPLOYMENTS_STAGE=dev
# GENTIAN_DEPLOYMENTS_PATH=${HOME}/.gentian/gentian-deployments
#
# The in-cluster App Store pushes install and uninstall commits to
# gentian-deployments through the operator lifecycle API. Set
# GENTIAN_DEPLOYMENTS_GIT_TOKEN in install.secrets.env for it — install.sh
# creates gentian-deployments-git-credentials in gentian-system — and enable
# appLifecycle.deployments in clusters/<cluster>/kernel/values.yaml.

# =============================================================================
# Where cluster configuration lives
# =============================================================================
# Cluster behavior is configured in gentian-deployments, not here:
#   clusters/<cluster>/kernel/claims/cluster.yaml    — the Cluster claim, and
#     the only place KERNEL_DOMAIN is authored; install.sh reads and writes it
#     there (see docs/deployment.md §1)
#   clusters/<cluster>/kernel/values.yaml            — cluster-unique Helm
#     overlay: image tag pins, cloudflare.zoneID / cloudflare.tunnelCNAME
#   profiles/<stage>.yaml                            — policy shared by every
#     cluster of that stage: log level, ACME issuer
#
# The Cluster claim schema covers the cluster's modes and sizing — routingMode,
# tenancyMode, networkMode, secretMode, storageClass, llm, tenantDefaults and
# mail. The values below feed the gentian-cluster-config ConfigMap that the
# Compositions read, so set them here; the claim fields the installer reads are
# kernelDomain, certManager.letsencryptEmail, certificates.issuerMode,
# openbao.* and oidc.discoveryUrl.
#
# claims/cluster.yaml carries what the cluster IS: tenancyMode, networkMode,
# nodeIp, routingMode, secretMode, storageClass, lbProvider, lbAnnotations, the
# mail block (serviceMode and, for an external relay, host/port/ssl/starttls)
# and the llm block. --prepare-deployment writes them and install.sh reads them
# back with yq before Crossplane exists.
#
# Setting any of them here instead is a mistake the installer now reports: this
# file is loaded first, so a value here silently overrode the claim and an
# operator editing claims/cluster.yaml saw no effect.
#
# Cloudflare splits three ways: the CF_API_TOKEN secret and the optional
# CF_ZONE_NAME verifier override go in install.secrets.env, and the operator's
# runtime metadata goes in clusters/<cluster>/kernel/values.yaml.
#
# KERNEL_DOMAIN is the one exception, and only on a brand-new cluster: the claim
# does not exist until install.sh creates it. Leave it unset to be prompted, or
# set it here to answer that prompt for a non-interactive run. install.sh reads
# claims/cluster.yaml on every run where it exists.
# KERNEL_DOMAIN=platform.example.com

# =============================================================================
# Installer behavior
# =============================================================================
# What the INSTALLER does, not what the cluster IS. Anything describing the
# cluster — its domain, its network and routing modes, its issuer, its mail
# mode, whether it serves models — belongs on the Cluster claim, which
# `./install.sh --prepare-deployment` scaffolds and you then edit. Setting one
# of those here silently beats the claim, and install.sh says so when it sees
# one.
# Set to 0 when cert-manager, CloudNativePG and Reloader are managed elsewhere
# in this cluster and install.sh should not install them.
INSTALL_CLUSTER_INFRA=1

# Set to 1 in CI to take the default for every prompt instead of asking.
GENTIAN_NONINTERACTIVE=0

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OpenBao CLI
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which `bao` binary to fetch when one is not on PATH. Install-time, not cluster
# state. Defaults to the pin in versions.yaml, which is the single place every
# component version is declared — set this only to install something else, and
# expect to explain why.
# OPENBAO_CLI_VERSION=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Container images
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# install.sh pulls published images; it does not build operator or portal images
# locally. gentian-os CI publishes a moving tag per published branch (develop,
# main, test-cb), the version for a release tag (v1.2.3 publishes as 1.2.3), and
# <branch>-<short-sha> for a commit — see .github/workflows/ci.yaml. gentian-ui
# tags portal images the same way, so set PORTAL_IMAGE_TAG to match gentian-ui.
# A cluster pins its tags in clusters/<cluster>/kernel/values.yaml.
#
# GENTIAN_OS_IMAGE_TAG is derived from GENTIAN_OS_BRANCH when left unset, so a
# cluster tracking a branch or a release runs the image published for it. Set it
# only to run something other than what the tracked ref published.
# GENTIAN_OS_IMAGE_TAG=develop
# GENTIAN_OS_IMAGE_PULL_POLICY=Always
# PORTAL_IMAGE_TAG=develop
# PORTAL_SKIP_IMAGE_BUILD=true

# ── Infra chart source ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where the installer fetches the infrastructure charts, and whether that
# registry needs credentials. Install-time, not cluster state: these decide what
# the INSTALLER does before a cluster exists, which is why they are here rather
# than on the Cluster claim alongside networkMode and nodeIp.
# INFRA_CHART_REPO=oci://registry.example.com/charts
# INFRA_CHART_PRIVATE=false
